// @emit-error emitProgram: index access but array type not collected
// A NAMED function with an UN-ANNOTATED return whose body returns an `f32[]` binding is
// an EMIT REJECT, while its `f64[]` and `i64[]` siblings — the same three-line shape with
// only the leaf type changed — both run:
//
//     function mk() { const xs: f64[] = [1.5]; return xs }   // prints 1.5
//     function mk() { const xs: i64[] = [6e9]; return xs }   // prints 6000000000
//     function mk() { const xs: f32[] = [1.5]; return xs }   // THIS FILE — rejects
//
// Found by GRADING A ZERO, not by a bug report. `emit_collect`'s A20 inferred-return
// chain has an `f32[]` arm (`fRetKind[li] = "f32list"`) that is reached only when the
// function has NO return annotation, and a sabotage that answers that arm WRONG is inert
// on every channel this compiler has: 1,487 corpus files (wasm sha256 + build status +
// diagnostics), 25,200 fuzz programs, and 24 hand-constructed candidate shapes. The
// comparator was live throughout — the same sweep fires on `valueAtomKind`'s and the
// `nulstr` arm's sabotages.
//
// This file is WHY the arm has no witness: its intended input never gets that far. The
// checker DOES record the name (`isScalarListRetName` admits `i64[]`/`f32[]`/`f64[]`,
// which is why that classifier's `f32` arm is live while its `f64` arm is not), but the
// f32 list's wrapper/backing types are never forced for this binding, so the read
// `mk()[0]` fails collection. So the A20 f32 arm is unreachable BEHIND a defect, not
// merely untested — the distinction a coverage zero alone cannot make.
//
// When the f32 list's types are forced for an inferred-return binding, this graduates to
// `@run` (expected `1.5`) and the A20 `f32[]` arm gets its first pin.
function mk() {
  const xs: f32[] = [1.5]
  return xs
}
function main() {
  print(mk()[0])
}
main()
